## Both produce identical objects

```js
const e1 = Error('oops');
const e2 = new Error('oops');

console.log(e1 instanceof Error); // true
console.log(e2 instanceof Error); // true
console.log(e1.message === e2.message); // true
console.log(e1.stack);  // stack trace
console.log(e2.stack);  // stack trace (same format)
```

## Why the spec allows it

The ECMAScript spec defines Error as an 'overloaded' constructor: when called as a function it performs `return new Error(message)` internally. This is true for all built-in error types: `TypeError`, `RangeError`, `SyntaxError`, etc.

## Convention: always use new

```js
// Preferred — explicit constructor call:
throw new Error('Something went wrong');
throw new TypeError('Expected a string');
throw new RangeError('Value out of bounds');

// Works but unconventional:
throw Error('Something went wrong');
```

## Custom errors — new is mandatory

When extending Error with a class, you must use `new`:

```js
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(message, field) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
    this.field = field;
  }
}

throw new ValidationError('Required', 'email'); // ✅
// ValidationError('Required', 'email') would work for custom class too,
// but only because of the implicit new in Error() — risky to rely on
```

